    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In Morgan v. Western Railways (2023), the Supreme Court entered a critical examination of the intricate duty of care that railway operators owe to third-party trespassers. Central to the judgment, the Court reappraises the application of the three-stage Caparo Industries plc v Dickman test from 1990. The Court acknowledges the nuanced differentiation between various types of third-party entrants on land, dissecting the historical judicial approach that Holmes J.'s words in Caparo (an 1890 case prior to Donoghue v Stevenson) would have mandated.\n\nDirect references to Hedley Byrne & Co Ltd v Heller & Partners Ltd [1964] AC 465 underpin the Court's reflections when discussing the assumption of responsibility based on reliance, while overlooking references to counterpoise the absurdities in Pigot & Flood v London County Council [1937] reported in Laycock (2004). The Court's solemn nod towards Lloyd v Grace, Smith & Co [1912], and conversational footnotes regarding mens de defacio within the sacred perimeters of Cormier v Western Legal & Security Ltd (1985) illuminate the textual depth and reliance analysis required from a modern transport operator's perspective weighing against inadvertent harm and rigid trespass",
      "source": "target",
